# Model Routing Workflow

The parent agent is an orchestrator. The preferred parent model is GPT-5.6 Luna.

## Classify the task

A task is trivial when all of the following are true:

- The intended change is obvious
- It is localized and low-risk
- It does not alter architecture, APIs, persistence, authentication,
  concurrency, security boundaries, or core behavior
- Acceptance can be verified with a focused command or inspection

The Luna parent may perform trivial work directly.

A task is nontrivial when it involves ambiguity, multiple modules, unfamiliar
code, architectural choices, external APIs, data models, security, concurrency,
migration, substantial debugging, or broad behavioral changes.

## Required workflow for nontrivial tasks

1. Before any file edits, invoke the sol-planner subagent.
2. Treat the resulting plan as the implementation contract.
3. Delegate each [LUNA] task to luna-worker.
4. Delegate each [TERRA] task to terra-implementer.
5. Do not run agents concurrently when their write scopes overlap.
6. After implementation, run appropriate tests and verification.
7. Invoke terra-reviewer on the complete final diff.
8. Send straightforward review fixes to luna-worker.
9. Send complex review fixes to terra-implementer.
10. Repeat the Terra review after blocking or important fixes.

## Sol escalation conditions

Invoke sol-planner again only when:

- Requirements materially change
- The discovered architecture contradicts the plan
- A public interface or data model must change unexpectedly
- Two implementation or repair attempts fail
- The reviewer identifies a fundamental design flaw

Do not use Sol for routine coding, test execution, formatting, documentation,
simple debugging, or ordinary review.

## Completion gate

Do not claim completion until:

- Required implementation exists
- Relevant verification has run
- Test results are reported honestly
- Terra reviewer has no unresolved blocking findings
- Remaining limitations are explicitly documented
